\Title {Dynamic Environment State Estimation for Multi-Agent Systems under Partial Macroscopic Observations}
\Author {Xinle Zhang}
\Language {en}
\Copyright {\textcopyright \ 2026. This work is licensed under a Creative Commons "Attribution-NonCommercial-ShareAlike 4.0 International" (BY-NC-SA 4.0) license.}
\CopyrightURL {https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/deed.en}
\Subject {Understanding the overall state of a pedestrian crowd is challenging when observations cover only parts of the environment. Robots in crowded public spaces have limited sensor coverage, making it difficult to estimate pedestrians outside their individual perception ranges. Multiple robots can share observations from different parts of the environment, as local, aggregated measurements such as pedestrian density and mean velocity. However, these measurements are incomplete, and reconstructing the global crowd state from them requires estimating both the full state of the crowd and the uncertainty associated with that estimate. Methods from data assimilation and sparse field reconstruction address such problems in other domains, but to the best of our knowledge, their performance on crowd state estimation has not been reported. This thesis addresses how accurately such methods reconstruct a crowd's macroscopic state from the partial observations of mobile robots, the quality of the uncertainty, and their computational cost. It compares a localised Ensemble Kalman Filter (EnKF), DINCAE, Senseiver and 4DVarNet using the ATC dataset, 92 days of real pedestrian data, observed by three robots. Two extensions are proposed: since Senseiver uses only the current frame, although most currently unobserved cells were observed seconds earlier, Senseiver-G aligns its latent representation with the grid and adds a causal window of past observations; since 4DVarNet provides no uncertainty, an augmented 4DVarNet refines the state and its log-variance jointly within its trained solver. Senseiver-G is the most accurate of the six methods on every state channel. On the 15 test days, its RMSE on the walkable cells, pooled over the four state channels, is 12.7 percent below that of the original Senseiver. Among the probabilistic methods, DINCAE gives the best predictive distribution, with the lowest continuous ranked probability score on every test day, helped by its accurate point estimate. The augmented 4DVarNet provides an uncertainty for a quarter more computation than the plain 4DVarNet but, in the reported run, is less accurate. The compute time per frame differs by more than two orders of magnitude between the methods and is far below the one-second observation interval, but it is not the end-to-end latency: it excludes input preparation and, for DINCAE and 4DVarNet, the wait for later observations. }
\Keywords {state estimation\sep uncertainty quantification\sep crowd state reconstruction\sep multi-robot systems\sep data assimilation\sep partial observations}
